-**Two assertions a blocklist cannot express** run on every scan: a document that carries a figure only in its split renderings (digits broken across tokens, a number spelled across a line break) fails; and a proper-noun-free paragraph that names three of the client's tool categories in one sentence fails, because the combination of a client's tools identifies them with every proper noun removed. The user's own firm name is a near-homophone of a common word, so the string list is backed by a **phonetic-variant rule** with a whitelist of legitimate uses and a test asserting no false positive on the ordinary word. The lint's tests feed it the full set of trap shapes (case-varied, possessive and comma-split organisation names, identifiers that appear only in a header, alias variants that are ordinary words, names harvested from the body, split dates, fused tokens, a vendor's name substituted for another's, and the rest) with a clean twin, so it is graded on false positives as well as misses.
